Z||000001||GREENDANO||07-18-99||07:45 AM||||I think you've been had by their "Snake Oil"<br>  The  Fiero ECM uses no time input related to the time of day/month.  It has an internal clock, so to speak, for driveability fuctions, but it doesn't have a "date" section to worry about.  Have you ever stored your car for the winter and disconneceted the battery?  How did you reset the "ECM clock"  ??  You didn't, because there isn't one.<br>   You did read the last bit about "BUYING ONE OF OUR OBD-II CONVERSIONS TO ELIMINATE THE Y2K BUG" on their sight, right?  HMMM?  Sounds like a sales pitch aimed at the Y2K survivalist.<br> I might try the Y2K bug line on my wife, though, maybe I can get that supercharged 3800 I've been eyeballing.  :)<br>   Dan-O
Z||000002||Cooter||07-18-99||08:11 AM||||Dan-O,<br>That is exactly what I was thinking! A computer is a terrible clock, most will gain/loose time if not reset once and a while. If the car computer suffered from the y2k bug, we would already have dead cars because some of the clocks would be far off enough to think that it was already y2000.
Z||000003||GREENDANO||07-18-99||07:57 PM||||All right....update time....Wife said , "No" to 3800. It had something to do with having a two month old 3.2 already in the car and the big dollar Pro-streeter parked next to it in the garage.  She's right, that's why I keep her around...   :)<br>  Dan-O
Z||000004||batboy||07-18-99||08:27 PM||||Well Dan-o, maybe you can at least talk her into letting you get a turbo for that 3.2 in your Fiero. As far as this Y2K thing goes, like any other computer, in order to hold the date and time, a car computer would need a small back-up battery. Of course they don't have this back-up battery, but if they did, all you would have to do is just take it out, let the computer go back to the default date, put it back in and go for several more years.
